WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:09.370
Imagine a desert almost as large as the U.S., and where it gets so hot you can fry

2
00:00:09.411 --> 00:00:10.391
an egg on the sand.

3
00:00:11.151 --> 00:00:13.992
Although it may be hard to picture, it actually does exist.

4
00:00:14.592 --> 00:00:20.674
Spanning the northern third of the African continent, the Sahara is the world's largest desert; it stretches from

5
00:00:20.814 --> 00:00:23.114
Africa's Atlantic coastline to the Red Sea.

6
00:00:23.794 --> 00:00:30.237
In this vast and desolate area, the sunsets are breathtaking, the vistas are magnificent, and the sun and

7
00:00:30.337 --> 00:00:31.558
wind are unforgiving.

8
00:00:32.438 --> 00:00:38.262
A desert is defined as an area with little or no rainfall, and in this respect, the Sahara

9
00:00:38.322 --> 00:00:39.083
is no exception.

10
00:00:39.963 --> 00:00:45.567
It averages a mere one inch of rainfall annually, and sometimes it receives no rain for years.

11
00:00:46.708 --> 00:00:52.913
There are, however, lush oases—supplied with water from underground aquifers—scattered throughout.

12
00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:59.505
Temperatures can reach as high as 55 degrees Celsius during the day and drop below 0 at night.

13
00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:06.228
Surprisingly, sand dunes cover only about a sixth of this desert; the rest is made up of mountain

14
00:01:06.268 --> 00:01:09.709
ranges, gravel plains, salt flats, and rock beds.

15
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.030
The Sahara wasn't always this way.

16
00:01:12.851 --> 00:01:19.393
About ten thousand years ago, the Sahara received much more rainfall and was much more densely populated than

17
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:20.054
it is today.

18
00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:24.896
The rains created suitable growing conditions for wildlife and human settlements.

19
00:01:25.716 --> 00:01:29.857
Hippos, giraffes, and even rhinoceroses roamed throughout the region.

20
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:38.400
However, about five thousand years ago, the rainfall lessened, the grasses died out, and the Sahara was transformed

21
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.961
from a green savanna to the infertile desert that exists today.

22
00:01:42.921 --> 00:01:49.363
Lack of water has created a harsh environment; nevertheless, hundreds of extremely diverse species live here.

23
00:01:49.923 --> 00:01:52.864
They have evolved special adaptations to help them survive.

24
00:01:53.596 --> 00:01:56.458
Insects are some of the most plentiful creatures in the Sahara.

25
00:01:57.038 --> 00:02:03.723
To cope with the desert's extreme temperatures, most species have a waxy covering to reduce moisture loss, and

26
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:07.645
many of them burrow in the ground during daytime, coming out to eat only at night.

27
00:02:08.246 --> 00:02:12.389
The Sahara scorpion is no different; it hides under rocks during the day.

28
00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:16.591
At night, it hunts, using the liquids from its prey to survive.

29
00:02:17.312 --> 00:02:24.064
Another insect, the brightly colored scarab beetle—it can be burgundy, copper, or mint green, among other shades—has a

30
00:02:24.084 --> 00:02:25.064
unique adaptation.

31
00:02:25.885 --> 00:02:30.446
It lays its eggs in other animals' dung, which it has collected and rolled into balls.

32
00:02:31.086 --> 00:02:37.568
This behavior protects the eggs from the Sahara's abrasive winds and scorching heat; it also provides nourishment for

33
00:02:37.608 --> 00:02:38.648
the scarab larvae.

34
00:02:39.389 --> 00:02:46.271
In ancient Egypt, people saw young beetles inexplicably emerging from the dung, and the scarab became associated with

35
00:02:46.331 --> 00:02:51.072
rebirth and the Egyptian god Khepri, who was responsible for the sun's daily rebirth.

36
00:02:52.032 --> 00:02:58.195
Given the enormous area of the Sahara, the number of reptile species there—only about one hundred—is relatively low.

37
00:02:59.075 --> 00:03:04.537
Reptiles such as snakes and lizards use many of the same methods as insects to survive the desert.

38
00:03:05.137 --> 00:03:09.279
For instance, they lie in the shade offered by small rocks to keep cool.

39
00:03:09.859 --> 00:03:13.381
But some species have developed other, more original adaptations.

40
00:03:14.061 --> 00:03:20.184
The sandfish (a type of lizard) has super-smooth scales that allow it to "swim" through sand in search

41
00:03:20.204 --> 00:03:20.744
of insects.

42
00:03:21.532 --> 00:03:25.613
Its fringed feet also help to propel it along below the surface of the sand.

43
00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:31.595
In the extreme Saharan heat, staying below the sand's surface reduces the lizard's exposure to the sun.

44
00:03:32.455 --> 00:03:37.517
The Sahara also has a rather small assortment of mammals, about seventy species in all.

45
00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:41.818
Over two-thirds of these are smaller animals, such as the fennec fox.

46
00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:48.968
This species' thick, sand-colored fur helps protect it from extreme temperatures; even the soles of its feet are

47
00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.469
covered in fur so it can walk on the hot sand.

48
00:03:52.089 --> 00:03:58.071
It also possesses big ears, which help dissipate heat and allow the fennec fox to hear insects moving

49
00:03:58.151 --> 00:03:58.791
on the sand.

50
00:03:59.511 --> 00:04:04.773
Of the large mammals in the Sahara, antelopes, gazelles, camels, and hyenas are most common.

51
00:04:05.513 --> 00:04:10.775
One of the most adaptive large animals is the addax, a member of the antelope family, which can

52
00:04:10.815 --> 00:04:15.756
go without drinking for several years because it gets its water from the plants it eats.

53
00:04:16.448 --> 00:04:21.530
To find shelter from the heat and sandstorms, the addax digs holes in the sand dunes with its

54
00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:22.931
large, flat hooves.

55
00:04:23.771 --> 00:04:29.154
It also uses camouflage to help it survive; babies are born the same color as the sand and

56
00:04:29.254 --> 00:04:32.515
stay that shade until they are strong enough to travel with the herd.

57
00:04:33.235 --> 00:04:35.877
Birds use camouflage to avoid predators as well.

58
00:04:36.437 --> 00:04:43.060
The Houbara bustard, a large bird with a wingspan of 140 centimeters, hunts in the rocky crags of

59
00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.598
the Sahara, where its speckled plumage helps it blend in among the rocks as it searches for rodents

60
00:04:47.658 --> 00:04:48.138
and lizards.

61
00:04:48.878 --> 00:04:56.401
The sandgrouse's coloring also acts as camouflage, but this bird has another adaptation: special feathers on its chest

62
00:04:56.461 --> 00:04:59.022
and belly that can soak up water at watering holes.

63
00:04:59.703 --> 00:05:03.864
Adult birds use these feathers to bring water for their chicks from distant oases.

64
00:05:04.845 --> 00:05:11.368
In the harsh environment of the desert, it is remarkable that 2.5 million people live there—mostly dwelling on

65
00:05:11.388 --> 00:05:13.913
the coasts or leading a nomadic lifestyle.

66
00:05:14.633 --> 00:05:16.114
The most notable are the Tuareg.

67
00:05:16.674 --> 00:05:24.217
Until a century ago, they controlled most of the major trans-Sahara trading routes—back when gold, ivory, and slaves

68
00:05:24.237 --> 00:05:25.197
were in heavy demand.

69
00:05:26.018 --> 00:05:31.360
They are known for wearing the "tagelmust," a long piece of blue cloth wrapped into a turban and

70
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:37.982
veil. The tagelmust protects their faces from the sun, sandstorms, and the infamous Saharan winds.

71
00:05:39.063 --> 00:05:42.904
All Tuareg men over the age of 25 must wear it—even when sleeping.

72
00:05:43.665 --> 00:05:49.607
The indigo dye used to make the veil eventually wears off, rubbing into the skin and giving the

73
00:05:49.647 --> 00:05:51.848
Tuareg their nickname: "the blue people."

74
00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:56.950
The Tuareg have survived by adapting their needs to what little the desert can give them.

75
00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:05.094
Their traditional nomadic existence involves traveling by camel caravan accompanied by their livestock, and living in tents made

76
00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:06.914
out of camel skins or goat hair.

77
00:06:07.454 --> 00:06:10.916
They eat mostly meat and cheese made from camel or goat milk.

78
00:06:11.532 --> 00:06:14.434
But recent droughts have forced the Tuareg to change their lives.

79
00:06:15.175 --> 00:06:20.459
Many of these people have traded their traditional tents and nomadic ways for mud-and-brick homes or farms.

80
00:06:21.219 --> 00:06:25.222
One could say this new way of life is simply another adaptation to a changing world.

81
00:06:26.043 --> 00:06:31.747
Part of that change includes one of the Sahara's most pressing issues: the spread of the desert, or

82
00:06:31.767 --> 00:06:38.452
"desertification." The Sahara Desert is expanding southward at a rate of about five to ten kilometers a year,

83
00:06:39.144 --> 00:06:42.345
and sand is encroaching on many communities and farming regions.

84
00:06:43.026 --> 00:06:47.267
This is specifically a concern for the fragile savannas of sub-Saharan Africa.

85
00:06:47.908 --> 00:06:50.829
Desertification is intensified by human neglect.

86
00:06:51.529 --> 00:06:57.512
When farmers abandon crop fields for more fertile ground, the plant roots no longer hold down the soil

87
00:06:57.872 --> 00:07:00.693
and the desert quickly moves in, making the land unusable.

88
00:07:01.753 --> 00:07:08.913
From prehistoric times to the present, a variety of species and nomadic communities have survived by adapting to

89
00:07:08.953 --> 00:07:12.055
the desolation and extreme conditions of the Sahara Desert.

90
00:07:12.816 --> 00:07:16.499
Surviving climate change may turn out to be their greatest challenge yet.
